ged or missed incorrectly, your rule is NOT READY.**  \n        - Revise: Make boundaries stricter, split the rule, or drop steps not justified *everywhere* by the outputs.\n    - **Reject any \u201cgeneralization,\u201d merging, or filling that isn\u2019t demanded by *all* examples.** Never use bounding boxes, shortcut fills, or inferred region merges unless every output proves it\u2014*ambiguity means restriction is required*.\n\n---\n\n**3. Boundary-First Feature Handling (Critical Constraint)**\n\n- **Treat boundaries (edges, connectors, blobs, region contours) as *absolute*:**  \n    - **Never fill, overlay, color across a connector, line, or feature separation unless *all* outputs show this for *every* similar case.**\n    - Where features could be merged or left separate, **if any ambiguity exists across outputs, keep them separate**\u2014no overmerging.\n    - Where \u201cflood-filling\u201d or \u201cconnecting,\u201d specify *explicitly* the cell-by-cell stop rule (e.g., \u201cflood only within regions \u22652 cells thick\u201d, or \u201cstop at any pixel with \u22642 non-background neighbors\u201d, etc.). Do NOT allow color bleed.\n\n---\n\n**4. Conservative Rule Verification (Change-By-Change, Not By Heuristic)**\n\n- **Simulate your rules exactly on every training grid:**  \n    - **Confirm:** *Every* transformed cell in the output is correctly produced\u2014NO more, NO less.\n    - *If even one example fails:*  \n        - Tighten your rule, restrict its scope, or create an exception\u2014never guess, extrapolate, or vote.\n        - Prove all unchanged regions/features remain *strictly* untouched.\n- *Prefer under-generalization:*  \n    - If you\u2019re unsure if a feature should be merged, colored, or extended, **default to not changing it.**\n    - *Every rule must be minimal:* Change nothing unless the output difference demands it, in the same spots, every time.\n\n---\n\n**5. Final Consistency Checklist Before Implementation**\n\n- *Directly compa